{
  "version": 1,
  "about": [
    "Cardinal numbers in nine languages. Five implementations read one grammar",
    "file (src/loudkit/models/data/numbers.json) and must produce these strings.",
    "",
    "Every expectation is hand-written from the language's own reference",
    "description, not captured from this implementation's output: a fixture that",
    "records what the code does proves only that the code is deterministic. Two",
    "real defects were caught this way while the file was being written — Danish",
    "*entusind* for 1000, and a listed Spanish *veintiuno* overriding the",
    "feminine *veintiuna*.",
    "",
    "The `gendered` section covers the values that agree with what is counted.",
    "Nothing here reads context: the caller supplies the gender, because it is a",
    "property of the sentence rather than of the number.",
    "French uses the 1990 rectified orthography (vingt-et-un, with hyphens), matching the CLDR corpus this grammar is differentially tested against. Both spellings are read identically aloud; consistency with the larger corpus decides the tie.",
    "fi/no/sv forms come from each language's own authority (Kotus/Kielikello for Finnish including the thousand-boundary space; Språkrådet's 1951 tens-first order with sju/tjue for Bokmål; SAOL/Isof for Swedish including fyrtio and the ettusen consonant reduction), cross-checked against CLDR. num2words ships förtio, etttusen and 'en tusen' — three more of the Danish-treds class this fixture exists to keep out."
  ],
